\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Relabeling transports the state and its marginal}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{Q>0} be a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}
on #{\mathbb R^V}, and let #{P:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^{V'}}
be the coordinate permutation induced by a bijection #{p:V\to V'}.
Put #{Q'=PQP^T}. Then #{Q'>0}, and the
\vocabk{Gaussian probability laws}{fdip-0005} satisfy
##{Z_{Q'}=Z_Q,\qquad P_*\mu_Q=\mu_{Q'}.}
Here the Borel pushforward is defined by
#{(P_*\mu)(A)=\mu(P^{-1}(A))} for every Borel set #{A}.
This extends the [finite pushforward convention](ftip-007S) to measures.
For every Borel function #{F':\mathbb R^{V'}\to\mathbb R},
##{\begin{aligned}
F'\in L^1(\mu_{Q'})
 &\Longleftrightarrow F'\circ P\in L^1(\mu_Q),\\
\langle F'\rangle_{Q'}
 &=\langle F'\circ P\rangle_Q.
\end{aligned}}
The second identity assumes the equivalent integrability conditions;
#{L^1} means that the absolute integral is finite. The
\vocabk{covariance}{fdip-0006} matrices obey #{G'=PGP^T}.
In particular these conclusions apply to an
\vocabk{exact relabeling}{fdip-000G} of finite Gaussian backgrounds.}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55}{friedli2017statistical}, p. 505, proves
the Gaussian change of variables. Here it specializes to a permutation,
whose absolute determinant is one.
\citet{Appendix A.5.5}{boyd2004convex}, pp. 650-651, supplies the block
convention for the [positive Schur complement](fdip-000D) below.}

\proof{
\p{For #{z\ne0}, one has #{P^Tz\ne0}, so
#{z^TQ'z=(P^Tz)^TQ(P^Tz)>0}. Under #{z=P\phi},
##{z^TQ'z=\phi^TQ\phi,\qquad dz=d\phi.}
[Coordinate permutations preserve product Lebesgue measure](fdip-002J); equivalently,
#{|\det P|=1}. Applying this substitution to
the nonnegative Gaussian weight gives #{Z_{Q'}=Z_Q}. For a Borel set
#{A\subseteq\mathbb R^{V'}}, applying it with an indicator gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\mu_{Q'}(A)\\
&\quad=\frac1{Z_Q}\int_{\mathbb R^V}
   \mathbf1_A(P\phi)e^{-\phi^TQ\phi/2}\,d\phi\\
&\quad=\mu_Q(P^{-1}(A)).
\end{aligned}}
This proves the pushforward identity.}

\p{Use the same nonnegative change of variables with #{|F'|} to get
##{\int |F'|\,d\mu_{Q'}=\int |F'\circ P|\,d\mu_Q,}
including when the value is infinite. This proves the integrability
equivalence. When finite, apply the substitution to the positive and
negative parts of #{F'} and subtract, giving the expectation identity.
Thus transporting an original observable #{F} by #{F'=F\circ P^T}
preserves its expectation. Finally, multiplying on either side shows
##{(PQP^T)^{-1}=PQ^{-1}P^T.}
The [inverse-precision covariance theorem](fdip-000B) identifies these
inverses with #{G'} and #{G}; the coordinate means remain zero.}
}

\p{Now take nonempty proper retained subsets #{R\subsetneq V} and
#{R'\subsetneq V'}, with complements #{U,U'}, and assume #{p(R)=R'}.
Let #{P_R,P_U} be the restrictions of the coordinate permutation. In
the grouped coordinates, #{P=\operatorname{diag}(P_R,P_U)}. If
#{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&H\\H^T&C\end{pmatrix}}, then
##{\begin{aligned}
A'&=P_RAP_R^T,\\
H'&=P_RHP_U^T,\\
C'&=P_UCP_U^T,\\
(C')^{-1}&=P_UC^{-1}P_U^T.
\end{aligned}}
Consequently the \vocabk{effective precisions}{fdip-000D} satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
S'&=A'-H'(C')^{-1}(H')^T\\
  &=P_R(A-HC^{-1}H^T)P_R^T\\
  &=P_RSP_R^T.
\end{aligned}}
Also #{|U'|=|U|} and #{\det C'=\det C}, since
#{(\det P_U)^2=1}. The entire integration factor
#{c_C=(2\pi)^{|U|/2}/\sqrt{\det C}} is therefore preserved.}

\p{For the coordinate projections #{\pi_R,\pi_{R'}}, the identity
#{\pi_{R'}P=P_R\pi_R} says that this square commutes:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  transport/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (v) at (0,0) {$\mathbb R^V$};
\node (vp) at (3.4,0) {$\mathbb R^{V'}$};
\node (r) at (0,-2) {$\mathbb R^R$};
\node (rp) at (3.4,-2) {$\mathbb R^{R'}$};
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[above=2mm] {$P$} (vp);
\draw[transport] (r) -- node[above=2mm] {$P_R$} (rp);
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[left=2mm] {$\pi_R$} (r);
\draw[transport] (vp) -- node[right=2mm] {$\pi_{R'}$} (rp);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The lower map exists because the retained subset is transported.
Pushforwards compose by taking inverse images of Borel sets. Combining
this square with the [marginal theorem](fdip-000E) therefore gives
##{\begin{aligned}
(P_R)_*\mu_S
 &=(P_R\pi_R)_*\mu_Q\\
 &=(\pi_{R'}P)_*\mu_Q\\
 &=(\pi_{R'})_*\mu_{Q'}=\mu_{S'}.
\end{aligned}}
Applying the first part to #{S'=P_RSP_R^T} also gives
#{Z_{S'}=Z_S}. Thus both the normalized retained law and the raw factor
#{c_C} respect this partition correspondence.}

\p{A global relabeling need not preserve a marginal requested on fixed
labels. In the [four-cycle](fdip-000F), take #{t=m=1} and retain
#{R=\{0,2\}}. Its effective precision is
##{S_{\mathrm{opp}}=
\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix}.}
Exchange labels #{1,2}, fixing #{0,3}, and form #{Q'=PQP^T}.
If the new retained labels are still #{\{0,2\}}, they select the
original adjacent variables #{\{0,1\}}. In the old coordinate order
#{(0,1)\mid(2,3)}, the relevant blocks are
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\-1&3\end{pmatrix},\\
H&=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\\
C^{-1}=HC^{-1}H^T
 &=\frac18\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\1&3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Subtraction gives
##{S_{\mathrm{adj}}=
\begin{pmatrix}21/8&-9/8\\-9/8&21/8\end{pmatrix}.}
The diagonal entries #{7/3} and #{21/8} differ, so no two-coordinate
permutation conjugates these precisions. There is also no map on the old
retained fields factoring #{\pi_RP} through #{\pi_R}: the fields
#{0} and #{e_1} have the same old projection, but their projections
after #{P} are #{0} and #{(0,1)}. Here #{e_1} is one at vertex #{1}
and zero elsewhere. Transporting the retained subset to
#{p(R)=\{0,1\}} in the new labels restores the square and the theorem.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving} supplies
volume preservation for a linear isometry equivalence of finite-dimensional
real inner-product spaces. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supplies Gaussian transport in covariance parameters; specialize its
mean and shift to zero, its linear map to #{P}, and its covariance to
#{Q^{-1}}. The inverse identity above and the
[precision-density identification](fdip-000B) align that result with
#{\mu_Q}. These ingredients do not include the declared partition or its
counterexample.}
